  "worked_solution": "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,\u03d5) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $\u03d5\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5.$",
